.\" xmap.3 — xmap library manual
.\"
.TH XMAP 3 "September 2026" "libxc" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
xmap \- thread-safe dynamically indexed pointer and string map
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <xmap.h>

void xmap_init(xmap_t *xm);
void xmap_destroy(xmap_t *xm);

/* Generic pointers */
void  xmap_insert(xmap_t *xm, void *data);
void *xmap_get(xmap_t *xm, size_t index);
int   xmap_exists(xmap_t *xm, size_t index);
void  xmap_erase(xmap_t *xm, size_t index);
void  xmap_erase_no_shift(xmap_t *xm, size_t index);

/* Strings (char *) */
void  xmap_strinsert(xmap_t *xm, const char *str);
char *xmap_strget(xmap_t *xm, size_t str_index);
int   xmap_strexists(xmap_t *xm, size_t str_index);
void  xmap_strerase(xmap_t *xm, size_t str_index);
void  xmap_strerase_no_shift(xmap_t *xm, size_t str_index);

/* Wide strings (wchar_t *) */
void      xmap_wcsinsert(xmap_t *xm, const wchar_t *str);
wchar_t  *xmap_wcsget(xmap_t *xm, size_t wstr_index);
int       xmap_wcsexists(xmap_t *xm, size_t wstr_index);
void      xmap_wcserase(xmap_t *xm, size_t wstr_index);
void      xmap_wcserase_no_shift(xmap_t *xm, size_t wstr_index);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xmap
library implements a dynamically growing pointer array with
thread-safe access through an internal pthread mutex.

In addition to storing arbitrary
.BR void *
pointers, the map maintains dedicated index lists for NUL-terminated
character strings and wide-character strings.

Strings inserted through
.BR xmap_strinsert ()
or
.BR xmap_wcsinsert ()
are duplicated by the library and are owned by the map.

The main storage array
.I xm->map
contains the stored objects. String and wide-string index arrays contain
references to entries in the main storage array.

All public API functions lock the map mutex internally unless explicitly
documented otherwise.

.SH INITIALIZATION
.SS xmap_init()
.B xmap_init()
initializes an
.BR xmap_t
object.

No dynamic memory is allocated until an item is inserted.

The map must be initialized before any other operation and must not be
reinitialized while it contains active resources.

.SS xmap_destroy()
.B xmap_destroy()
releases all resources associated with the map.

It frees the map storage, stored objects, string and wide-string index
arrays, and destroys the internal pthread mutex.

The map must not be used after
.BR xmap_destroy ().

.SH GENERIC POINTERS
.SS xmap_insert()
.B xmap_insert()
adds a pointer to the map.

The pointer is stored directly and is not copied.

Ownership of the pointer is transferred to the map. The pointer must
therefore refer to memory that can safely be released by the map when the
corresponding entry is erased or when the map is destroyed.

.SS xmap_get()
.B xmap_get()
returns the pointer stored at
.IR index .

If
.I index
is invalid or the corresponding slot contains
.BR NULL ,
.B xmap_get()
returns
.BR NULL .

.SS xmap_exists()
.B xmap_exists()
tests whether
.I index
contains a non-NULL pointer.

It returns 1 when the entry exists and 0 otherwise.

.SS xmap_erase()
.B xmap_erase()
removes the entry at
.IR index .

The stored object is freed and all subsequent entries are shifted one
position toward the beginning of the map.

Associated string and wide-string index lists are updated to reflect the
shift.

Invalid indices are silently ignored.

.SS xmap_erase_no_shift()
.B xmap_erase_no_shift()
removes the entry at
.I index
without shifting subsequent entries.

The stored object is freed and the map slot is set to
.BR NULL .

Invalid indices are silently ignored.

.SH CHARACTER STRINGS
.SS xmap_strinsert()
.B xmap_strinsert()
inserts a NUL-terminated character string.

The string is duplicated using
.BR strdup (3).
The duplicated string is owned by the map and is automatically freed when
the corresponding entry is erased or the map is destroyed.

.SS xmap_strget()
.B xmap_strget()
returns the character string associated with string index
.IR str_index .

The returned pointer refers to memory owned by the map and must not be
freed by the caller.

It returns
.B NULL
when the index is invalid or the corresponding entry does not exist.

.SS xmap_strexists()
.B xmap_strexists()
tests whether a character-string index is valid and contains an entry.

It returns 1 if the entry exists and 0 otherwise.

.SS xmap_strerase()
.B xmap_strerase()
removes the character-string entry at
.IR str_index .

The stored string is freed and subsequent map entries are shifted.

The associated string-index list is updated accordingly.

Invalid indices are silently ignored.

.SS xmap_strerase_no_shift()
.B xmap_strerase_no_shift()
removes a character-string entry without shifting subsequent map entries.

The stored string is freed and its underlying map slot is set to
.BR NULL .

Invalid indices are silently ignored.

.SH WIDE STRINGS
.SS xmap_wcsinsert()
.B xmap_wcsinsert()
inserts a NUL-terminated wide-character string.

The string is duplicated using
.BR wcsdup (3).
The duplicated string is owned by the map and is automatically freed when
the entry is erased or the map is destroyed.

.SS xmap_wcsget()
.B xmap_wcsget()
returns the wide-character string associated with
.IR wstr_index .

The returned pointer is owned by the map and must not be freed by the
caller.

It returns
.B NULL
when the index is invalid or the corresponding entry does not exist.

.SS xmap_wcsexists()
.B xmap_wcsexists()
tests whether a wide-string index contains an entry.

It returns 1 if the entry exists and 0 otherwise.

.SS xmap_wcserase()
.B xmap_wcserase()
removes a wide-string entry and frees the stored string.

Subsequent map entries and associated wide-string indices are shifted as
necessary.

Invalid indices are silently ignored.

.SS xmap_wcserase_no_shift()
.B xmap_wcserase_no_shift()
removes a wide-string entry without shifting subsequent map entries.

The stored wide string is freed and the underlying map slot is set to
.BR NULL .

Invalid indices are silently ignored.

.SH MEMORY MANAGEMENT
The map owns all objects inserted through its ownership-taking interfaces.

The library frees:
.IP \(bu 2
objects inserted with
.BR xmap_insert ();
.IP \(bu 2
strings duplicated by
.BR xmap_strinsert ();
.IP \(bu 2
wide strings duplicated by
.BR xmap_wcsinsert ();
.IP \(bu 2
the map storage;
.IP \(bu 2
the string and wide-string index arrays.
.PP
Pointers returned by the
.BR xmap_get (),
.BR xmap_strget (),
and
.BR xmap_wcsget ()
functions remain owned by the map and must not be freed by the caller.

.SH THREAD SAFETY
All public
.B xmap
operations are thread-safe and lock the internal map mutex.

Internal or
.B _nolock
variants do not acquire the mutex. The caller must already hold
.IR xm->mutex
when using such functions.

The caller must not destroy a map while another thread is operating on
it.

.SH C++ BINDINGS
When compiled as C++, the header provides C++ convenience interfaces
including typed iterators, typed pointer retrieval, and helpers for
.B std::string
and
.BR std::wstring .

The C++ interfaces use appropriate C++ casts and provide string
convenience operations while retaining the ownership semantics of the
underlying map.

.SH RETURN VALUES
.B xmap_get(),
.B xmap_strget(),
and
.B xmap_wcsget()
return the requested pointer on success and
.B NULL
if the requested entry does not exist.

.B xmap_exists(),
.B xmap_strexists(),
and
.B xmap_wcsexists()
return 1 when the requested entry exists and 0 otherwise.

Insertion and erase functions return no value.

.SH ERRORS
The following errors may be reported through
.IR errno :
.TP
.B ENOMEM
A memory allocation failed while growing the map or duplicating a string.

.PP
Invalid indices passed to erase operations are silently ignored.

.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <xmap.h>

int
main(void)
{
    xmap_t xm;

    xmap_init(&xm);

    xmap_strinsert(&xm, "hello");
    xmap_strinsert(&xm, "world");

    printf("%s\\n", xmap_strget(&xm, 0));

    xmap_destroy(&xm);

    return 0;
}
.fi
.PP
The example prints:
.nf
hello
.fi

